WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.630
We as a species are living longer.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.620
Right now, the average American
lives for about 80 years.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.890
And the question is,
how long could we live?

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.590
We actually don't know the answer to that.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.070
This question is at the center

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.700
of the burgeoning field
of longevity science,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.840
and biotech startups believe
we are closer than ever

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.883
to unprecedented lifespan.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.180
A recent explosion of longevity startups

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.890
has created a new speculative
industry almost overnight.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.040
Oxford economics estimated longevity

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.803
to be worth $7.6 trillion.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:42.090
In order to reach its potential, right,

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.250
really needs to have a way
of getting to the market

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.357
as fast as we can possibly do it.

00:00:48.357 --> 00:00:50.760
[Narrator] But some fear
whatever breakthrough may happen

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.400
will never really serve
the average person.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.300
Rather, it will favor the few
who are sinking their fortunes

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.563
into these startups.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.340
It's a pretty concentrated
group of investors.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.500
From our estimates,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.270
it's like nearly 50% has
come from individuals.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.390
That's a nicer way of saying billionaires.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.710
I have to be honest,

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:15.640
a large part is egoistic
because the billionaires

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.330
they are getting older and
they have a lot of money

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.860
and they still have a lot of ambitions.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.090
They want to live longer
when they age better.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.437
When people think about longevity,

00:01:27.437 --> 00:01:30.740
the thing that comes to
mind is usually, you know,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.960
some eccentric billionaire
trying to live forever.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.160
But what longevity really is

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.810
is just the science of
helping us live longer.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:45.010
And also the science of helping
us live more healthfully.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.650
Anything that comes out of this space

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.793
won't be just for the rich.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.800
Humans have been trying to cheat death

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.653
since we climbed down from the trees.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.750
The Fountain of Youth was a mythical place

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.630
where you could drink
waters from its springs

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.720
to rejuvenate your aging body.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.620
It first appeared in writing
around the fifth century BC

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.573
and the search hasn't stopped since.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.150
Folklore has it that Spanish
Explorer Ponce de Leon searched

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.350
in vain for magical waters
with restorative powers

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.290
in modern day Florida and The Bahamas.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.430
But historians agree that most of his time

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:31.930
was actually spent searching

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.203
for something a bit more earthly.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.930
Not only was he searching for gold,

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.760
but expeditions like his were
expensive and in constant need

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.310
of fundraising from the
throne across the Atlantic.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.740
Today, the modern quest
for prolonged youth

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.503
remains very expensive.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.330
Last year, more than $2
billion in venture funding

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:03.930
went into the longevity space.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.960
So this is a space that
people are excited about

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.593
and they are putting their money into it.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.670
But this expedition is funded mostly

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.573
by a different sort of royalty.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.040
We have heard recently, you
know, that famous people,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.570
such as Jeff Bezos
invested, not only millions,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.240
but actually billions into
new startup companies.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.160
And I think you know,

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.770
it is totally justified this investment.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.660
While this narrative of this super rich

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.440
making an elixir of life for
themselves at the expense

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:39.900
of the rest of the world world makes

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.170
for good Bond villain material,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.000
it doesn't hold up to close scrutiny.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:44.833
I love it.

00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:48.330
The narrative about lots of billionaires

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.350
coming into this longevity space,

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:54.100
it's played up more than it
needs to be because in general,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.710
like VC funds are backed by billionaires

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:59.230
or families of billionaires.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.260
This is sort of the artery

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:05.490
of the the worldwide innovation economy

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.860
is people who have the
capital on hand to be able

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:12.160
to do these big, sometimes
risky, investments.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.060
I don't think that
impression is entirely wrong

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.730
but it's also not fully correct.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:20.740
And it really fails to
recognize the ways in which

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.980
the edges of human knowledge and capacity

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.870
are always explored on the outskirts

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.750
by a handful of individuals

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.623
who are uniquely motivated
and uniquely resourced.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.350
It never happens with
technology that somehow that

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.810
that then stays secretive

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.813
and closed up in a box and
only effective for them.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.920
As it turns out the
science of pharmacology

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.380
and even the basic laws
of supply and demand

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.033
don't really allow for
an elite super drug.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.840
That first pill that you
create or that first injection

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.927
or whatever it is that
might cost, you know,

00:04:59.927 --> 00:05:03.250
$500 million to make that first pill.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.870
The second one probably
cost a fraction of a penny.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.020
And so if you were to do the math

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.250
of like the supply and
demand aspects of this,

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:16.860
it doesn't make sense to make
a really expensive medicine

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.730
that only a few billionaires take

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:20.400
when you can make something really cheap

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.723
but make it for everybody.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:25.870
And there's another reason

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.340
why private billionaires and
VC's are flooding this space.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.970
As it turns out, longevity
health doesn't exactly fit

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.940
into the current healthcare model

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.930
making it risky and more complicated

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.320
to traditional pharmaceutical investors.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.680
The major regulatory
bodies that look at states

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.250
of disease, so the CDC, the FDA,

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.730
they don't consider death an illness.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:52.730
Of all of the clinical endpoints
that we generally look at

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.770
therapeutics alleviating
the suffering of humans,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.720
interestingly, death isn't one of them.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.300
Many people, very accurately,

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.840
particularly as we live
longer and healthier lives,

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.510
are pausing and saying, well, wait,

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.100
why do I wait to get involved

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.360
with the healthcare system until I'm sick?

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.290
And why is there no baseline
of what being healthy is?

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:17.700
There have been a lot of
people with resources,

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.190
i.e. billionaires, who are saying,

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.690
I know the best practices for
living long and healthy lives

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.630
but what are the treatments
to alleviate some

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.670
of these symptoms of aging?

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.875
And all the classic
investors in the space said,

00:06:30.875 --> 00:06:33.670
I don't really know how to fathom that.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:34.820
I don't know what to do

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.800
with these kind of soft
clinical endpoints.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.010
And I absolutely don't know
how to invest on the metric

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.400
of you make an investment today,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.750
you engage with a therapeutic practice,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:47.610
and then hopefully we find out in 50 years

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.860
that you lived 10 healthier years

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.070
than you might have otherwise, right?

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.570
That feedback loop is way too long.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.470
And that's really what's
enabled investors like ourselves

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.930
to start participating in this market.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.180
What's more, longevity science itself

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:07.790
challenges much of the healthcare
status quo as we know it.

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.160
Everyone in the space agrees
that for real longevity

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.390
to be achieved, there needs to
be a massive paradigm shift.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:18.680
We are locked into a system where we wait

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.880
for people to get sick and
then try to treat them.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:25.360
And that system worked really
well a hundred years ago

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:26.520
when infectious diseases

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.560
were the cause of death for everybody.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.225
If you come in with a heart attack

00:07:30.225 --> 00:07:32.810
or with stage three cancer

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.560
or with early stage Alzheimer's disease,

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.290
so much has already
broken down in our bodies

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.840
by the time that that doctor
is seeing that patient

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:45.040
that it is so complex to
imagine how we would fix all

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.420
of the pieces that have come apart.

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:51.620
All of these future technologies,
these future medicines,

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.580
they're built around
this idea of prevention.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.420
The notion that we could
switch from a healthcare system

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.740
that is primarily reactive,
wait for someone to get sick,

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.140
to one that is more proactive, right?

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.400
Give someone a drug so that
they could then stay healthy.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.350
So a more technically accurate
term for a longevity drug

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.010
would be a multi-disease preventative,

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.460
something that can keep
you from getting cancer

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.800
or Alzheimer's disease
or having a heart attack.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.050
The amazing potential of this science

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:23.890
is that we could have a single drug

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.330
that makes all of those things happen

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.510
further in the future than
they would otherwise have.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.770
As of today, a single
longevity drug remains

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:39.410
as real as the Fountain
of Youth and experts agree

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:41.290
that regardless of the technology,

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:44.460
longevity is a multi-pronged
effort using everything

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:48.520
from biomarkers and cellular
rejuvenation to gene editing,

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.310
diet, and exercise.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.593
There will likely never be a
one-size-fits-all solution.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.040
And to accomplish this

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.190
longevity startups are taking
an interdisciplinary approach

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:00.183
to the science.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:04.650
Scientists have really
started breaking down all

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:07.440
of the little things that
happen to us as we age.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.100
And there are a lot of them,

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:13.020
but I like to break them
down into three categories.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.730
Our top category is stuff that happens

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:17.500
at the whole tissue level.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.050
We call it like changes in how
our tissues are put together.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:25.050
We have stem cells that
get exhausted as we age

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.410
and now they can't make
new copies of themselves.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:30.040
And then if you zoom in into the cell,

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.850
there are changes that happen
at the molecular level,

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:36.150
specific molecules that
can't be broken down

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.980
by our normal recycling
mechanisms that build up

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:40.630
little by little as we get older.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:44.270
The business model of these startups

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:47.760
largely mirror this diverse
approach, casting a wide net

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.643
across both academic
science and pharmacology.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.290
It's kind of a new model

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:57.430
where you don't focus
on one single technology

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.880
or one single therapeutic area

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.330
because longevity is a
very complex approach

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.460
where many biological mechanisms

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.933
are interacted and intertwined.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:13.450
So we'd like to have the
scientists interacting

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:14.283
with each other

00:10:14.283 --> 00:10:17.183
even though they have a
specific scientific fields.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.650
We have like an incubator space.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:26.880
We bring in a cutting-edge science.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.880
We collaborate with academic
leaders in the field

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:34.940
and we bring in scientific entrepreneurs

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.220
to bring this breakthrough
science into our labs

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.453
here in Zurich.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.810
So we have labs and offices

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:48.750
and then we have very
experienced traditional pharma.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.490
People who know how to
discover and develop drugs

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.483
in this completely novel field.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.950
Where we find a great scientist

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:00.770
who has made a great discovery,
bring it into Cambrian,

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:05.070
and then use the fact that
we have a team of chemists

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:08.020
and drug developers and
researchers, and you know,

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.760
people who understand the
biotech market and all of this

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.963
to foster that internally.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.530
This aggregation model helps biotechs

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:20.113
hedge their bets against an
admittedly high-risk venture.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.530
Drug development is risky, right?

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:27.990
So when you start a drug in
clinical trials or human trials,

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:32.590
you only have about a one
in seven chance of that drug

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.400
making it all the way through.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.560
It's three phases of
clinical trials to approval.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:42.200
But if you pull together
20 of these different ideas

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:47.055
into one entity and resource them all,

00:11:47.055 --> 00:11:51.440
then all of a sudden that risk
goes up to about a 95% chance

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:52.960
that you'll get at least one hit.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:57.400
And so the math of drug
development makes a lot more sense

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.443
as a collective than as individuals.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.660
Even with so many bets on the table,

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.020
there's no guarantee that
a breakthrough will happen

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:09.203
under their roof.

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.780
Recently, a study funded
through traditional grants

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.130
out of the Salk Institute
successfully extended

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:16.760
the lifespan of mice.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.260
And while mouse studies are not

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.150
considered breakthrough discoveries,

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.110
it has gotten the attention
of the scientific community.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:26.545
We developed an approach
called partial reprogramming.

00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:29.076
Basically where we express a set of genes

00:12:29.076 --> 00:12:32.530
which can actually reprogram the cells

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.313
without losing their identity.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.980
We were able to erase the signs of aging.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:43.210
We were able to extend
its life by 30% almost.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.140
So basically this kind of demonstrates

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:50.140
that partial programming,
there is a way to rework some

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:54.040
of the aging marks in cells and tissues

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.920
and which can eventually
have a beneficial effect

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:59.359
on the whole organism.

00:12:59.359 --> 00:13:03.490
The partial program approach
was even tested in human cells.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.020
So we were able to reword, take cells

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:10.090
from an aged individual and
apply these partial programming

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:13.360
and erase these signs
of aging in the cells.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:17.233
So the conceptually, it
works even in human cells.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.020
The keyword here is conceptually.

00:13:22.323 --> 00:13:25.340
The science is in such early stages

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.250
and it grows every day.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.600
What we have is all of these
really interesting data points,

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.140
right, from human studies
from mouse studies

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.530
but we're many, many
years, if not decades away

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.580
from concrete answers that tell us how

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:43.533
these things contribute to how we age.

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:48.640
And this speaks to another misconception

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.070
about longevity.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.590
This community as a whole,
isn't so much focused

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:55.320
on increasing our lifespan
as much as they are

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:59.060
on improving our quality
of life as we age.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.260
It's not our aim and I don't think anyone

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.380
can prevent death, right?

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:07.120
Ultimately we will still all die,

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:11.060
but our aim is that until we die,

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:14.123
that we have the best
life that we can have.

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.570
In the longevity world,

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:19.070
almost nobody's talking about lifespan.

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:21.470
They're all talking about health span,

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.840
the amount of time that
we spend in good health.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.630
And if we prioritize
expanding health span,

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:31.250
increasing lifespan may
be a side effect of that.

